### Quickstart



```
import Ravensight from 'ravensight';

const rs = new Ravensight({
  ingestKey: 'gt_pub_your_public_key',
  gameVersion: '1.4.0',
});

// Reads the server kill switch and opens a session. Call it once at startup.
await rs.init();

rs.track('level_completed', { level: 3, deaths: 2, seconds: 91 });

// Optional: player feedback from an in-game form.
await rs.submitFeedback('The boss fight is too long', 'suggestion', 4);
```



A key in a web page is public, because anyone can read it from the page: in a browser use a public key (`gt_pub_`) with an event allowlist and a monthly ceiling, and keep `gt_live_` keys on your own server, such as a Node process. See [Public builds](https://www.ravensight.io/docs/public-builds/).



In a browser the SDK sends `game_started` and `game_exited` for you; pass `autoEvents: false` to send your own.



If your game shares a site with public pages, create the client only inside the game, never at app start: a client created on a landing page counts every visitor as a player. See [Web games: your website and your game](https://www.ravensight.io/docs/web-games/).



In CommonJS, use `const Ravensight = require('ravensight');`. Events go out in batches on a timer and when the page is hidden, so you never have to call `flush()` yourself. A Node process should `await rs.dispose()` before it exits, so the last batch goes out.

### Node servers with many players



The server takes the device id from the session, so every device needs its own session. The SDK keeps one per device id for you. On a game server, create one client and pass each player's id as the third argument to `track()`: a pseudonymous id your game generates, never an email or account name. Node has no `localStorage`, so pass an explicit `MemoryStorage` to keep the server's own id out of any shared storage.



```
import Ravensight, { MemoryStorage } from 'ravensight';

const rs = new Ravensight({
  ingestKey: process.env.RAVENSIGHT_KEY,
  gameVersion: '1.4.0',
  platform: 'dedicated-server',
  storage: new MemoryStorage(),
});
await rs.init();

function onMatchFinished(player, match) {
  rs.track('match_finished', { mode: match.mode, seconds: match.seconds }, { deviceId: player.analyticsId });
}

// On shutdown, the last flush for every player at once.
process.on('SIGTERM', async () => {
  await rs.dispose({ timeoutMs: 5000 });
  process.exit(0);
});
```



- **One request is one device.** A flush sends the run of queued events that share the device id at the head of the queue, up to 50, then the next run. Interleaved players cost more requests than one player, but nothing is ever sent under the wrong id.

- **Sessions are cached per id**, up to `maxSessions`. Past that the least recently used is dropped and reopens on that player's next event. Raise it if more players than that are active at once.

- **Limits are per game, shared by every player** on the client: 60 session creations and 600 tracked events a minute. A burst past that gets a `429`, and the SDK holds the events and retries. See [Usage, billing and limits](https://www.ravensight.io/docs/limits/).

- **`dispose()` is the last flush.** Call it on shutdown. Anything it cannot send inside `timeoutMs` stays in the queue file for the next start and is counted as `kept`, or as dropped with `persistQueue: false`.

### Joining the browser's session



When the same player also runs the browser SDK, the browser can hand its session to your server, so server-side events land in the same analytics session as the player's own, under the browser's device id.



```
// Browser: send the token to your server with your own request.
await fetch('/api/link-analytics', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ rsToken: rs.sessionToken }),
});

// Server: one call per player, before that player's next event.
rs.joinSession(body.rsToken, { deviceId: player.analyticsId });
rs.track('purchase_verified', { sku: 'gold_pack' }, { deviceId: player.analyticsId });
```



The next session opened for that id sends the token once as `joinToken`. The server answers with a session of its own that shares the browser's session id and device id, and never lives longer than the browser's. The local id is only the key the SDK caches that session under. If the server refuses the token, the SDK opens a plain session instead and records `invalid_join_token` in `stats.lastError`. Only a live session of the same game can be joined. A join from a playtest session stays synthetic, so the game server's events are marked as the playtest's too.

### Start the game SDK only inside the game



In a single page app, decide on every route change which tracker runs. Create the game client the first time a signed in player reaches a game route, never at app start and never on a public route. Run the website tracker on public routes and destroy it before a game route. Leaving the game for a public page keeps the game client, so the player's session carries on when they come back.



```
import Ravensight, { createSiteTracker } from 'ravensight';

let site = null;  // the website tracker, public pages only
let game = null;  // the game SDK, created once, inside the game only

// Your own rule for which routes are the game.
const isGameRoute = (path) => path === '/play' || path.startsWith('/play/');

// Call this from your router after every completed navigation.
export async function onRoute(path, account) {
  if (isGameRoute(path) && account) {
    if (site) { site.destroy(); site = null; }
    if (!game) {
      game = new Ravensight({ ingestKey: 'gt_pub_your_public_key', gameVersion: '1.4.0' });
      await game.init();
    }
    // A random id stored with the account, never a sequential one: see below.
    await game.identify(account.analyticsId);
  } else if (!isGameRoute(path) && !site) {
    site = createSiteTracker({ key: 'gt_site_your_website_key' });
  }
}
```



- **Never create the game client to "warm it up".** Creating it is what sends `game_started`, so a client built at app start counts the visitor even if nothing else is tracked.

- **Drop gameplay events from public pages.** If shared code tracks an event before the game client exists, discard it instead of queueing it for later: a queued visitor event would be sent under the next player.

- **Your sign in, sign up and account pages are public pages** for this purpose. The website tracker already leaves out sign in, account and receipt routes on its own; see [Connect a website](https://www.ravensight.io/docs/websites/#websites-setup) for its consent and excluded path options.

- **A game with no public pages** (the page is the game, as in a typical HTML5 export) needs none of this: create the client when the game starts.

### Identify on sign in, reset on sign out



A browser does not know who is playing, so without help the same person on a phone and a laptop counts twice, and two people on one browser count once. If your game has its own accounts, tell the SDK who signed in. This needs version 0.12.0 or later of the [ravensight package](https://www.npmjs.com/package/ravensight).



```
// After your own sign in succeeds, and at boot when a session is already signed in.
// analyticsId: a random UUID made once per account and stored with it.
await game.identify(account.analyticsId);

// When the player signs out.
await game.resetIdentity();
```



- **Pass a random, high-entropy id**, such as a random UUID or a random analytics id you make once for each account and store with it. Never pass a sequential database key, an email, a name or a handle: your game's salt reaches every browser that plays it, so anyone could hash 1, 2, 3 and onward and match a guessable id, while a random one cannot be guessed. A value containing `@`, an empty value and a value over 256 bytes are refused, and `identify` rejects with a `TypeError`, so await it or catch it.

- **Only a salted hash leaves the browser.** The SDK hashes the id with your game's own salt and sends the hash once per session. The raw id is never sent or stored. The hash still identifies one player across sessions, so it is a pseudonymous identifier: declare it in your game's privacy notice.

- **Each identified session uses one event** from your included allowance, then from your prepaid balance. The same account again in the same session sends nothing and uses nothing. With an empty balance the request is refused, costs nothing, and is tried again at the next session.

- **`resetIdentity()` also switches the browser to a fresh device id**, so the next person on that browser is not linked to the last one.



Every Ravensight SDK has the same pair of calls under its own language's spelling; see the page for your engine.

### Privacy: two controls that do different things



`setTrackingEnabled(false)` is the player's choice. It is saved in `UserDefaults` before the call returns, works before `start`, clears the queue and stops every request on this launch and every later one. `stopAllTelemetry()` is a stop for this run only: nothing is saved, the queue and the persisted queue file are cleared, `track` returns false, and feedback and reports are refused. The difference that matters is the playtest run: a synthetic session is driven by an agent on a test build, not by a player, so it ignores the saved opt-out, and only `stopAllTelemetry()` silences it.



| Call | Saved | Normal session | Synthetic (playtest) session |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `setTrackingEnabled(false)` | Yes | Queue cleared, no request of any kind, now and on every later launch. | Ignored, with one console line. The run carries on. |
| `stopAllTelemetry()` | No | Everything stops for this run. The next launch starts normally. `resumeTelemetry()` undoes it, and a saved opt-out still wins. | The run stops sending. |
| `resetDeviceId()` | Yes | A new durable id is saved and returned, the queue is cleared and the session ends. | A new durable id is saved for the next normal launch. The run keeps its playtest id. |



An analytics switch in your settings screen uses both:



```
func setAnalytics(_ on: Bool) {
    Ravensight.setTrackingEnabled(on)     // saved: holds on every later launch
    if on {
        Ravensight.resumeTelemetry()
    } else {
        Ravensight.stopAllTelemetry()     // also stops a playtest run, right now
    }
}
```

### Player Reports with a screenshot



`submitReport` sends what a player wrote about a problem, with your own metadata and, only if you pass one, a screenshot. The SDK never captures the screen: capture it yourself after the player chose to attach it, with anything private hidden. On macOS pass `screenshotData:` with JPEG, PNG or WebP bytes. Reports land in the dashboard's Player Reports inbox.



```
// A failed report kept for a retry, with the session it was sent under.
var pendingReport: (report: RavensightReport, session: String?)?

// Build the report once: its clientId, capture time and screenshot bytes
// are fixed here, so resubmitting this value sends the same submission.
let report = RavensightReport(
    category: "bug",
    message: "The ferry never arrives",
    screenshot: capturedImage,          // UIImage?, nil for a text report
    metadata: ["zone": "harbour", "attempt": 3]
)
let session = Ravensight.sessionToken

Ravensight.submitReport(report) { result in
    switch result {
    case .success(let receipt):
        print("report \(receipt.reportId ?? receipt.id) saved")
    case .failure(let error)
        where error.code == "upload_failed" || error.code == "report_processing" || error.status == 0:
        // Retry later with the same value, and only while the session is the same one.
        pendingReport = (report, session)
    case .failure(let error):
        print("report refused: \(error.code)")
    }
}

// Later:
if let pending = pendingReport, pending.session != nil, pending.session == Ravensight.sessionToken {
    Ravensight.submitReport(pending.report) { _ in }
}
```



**What it costs.** A text report counts as 1 event on the shared event meter. A screenshot adds 20,000 events, reserved when the report is accepted, which is about $0.01 once the included events are spent. The SDK makes one attempt per step and never retries a report on its own, because a screenshot reserves usage.



**When to retry.** The server recognises a resubmission by the report's `clientId` within one session only. Retry only after `upload_failed`, `report_processing` or a status of 0, with the same `RavensightReport` value and the same session token as the failed attempt: the server finds the existing submission and the SDK carries on from where it stopped. A retry under a different session (after a session expiry, a timeout in the background, `resetDeviceId` or a relaunch) is a new submission, stored again, and a screenshot reserves its events again. The server allows two upload attempts per submission and releases an unfinished screenshot report after 15 minutes. Limits are checked on the device first: a message of 1 to 500 characters, a screenshot of at most 1,000,000 bytes and 4,000,000 pixels, scaled to fit 1600 by 1600. `submitFeedback` stays as the lighter text only call.

### Queued, accepted and delivered



`track` returning `true` means the event is in the queue on the device, nothing more. Delivery is reported separately: `Ravensight.stats` counts `queued`, `delivered` (events in batches the server answered `202` for), `pending`, `droppedEvents` and `restored`; `lastDelivery` is `.accepted`, `.failed(status:)` or `.offline`; and `onFlush` is called on the main queue after every request that carried events. The server's own `accepted` count in that `202` answer can be lower than the batch the SDK counts as delivered, because an event timestamped more than 90 days ago is dropped without failing the request (see the [HTTP API](https://www.ravensight.io/docs/http-api/)). Accepted events reach your reports after the server's insert buffer flushes, which takes a few seconds.



```
Ravensight.shared?.onFlush = { outcome in
    if outcome.succeeded {
        print("delivered \(outcome.sent), \(outcome.pending) still queued")
    } else {
        print("not delivered (status \(outcome.status)), will retry")
    }
}
```

### Example for Node



A complete sender for Node 18 or newer in one file. It has no dependencies and uses only the built-in `https` module.



- It keeps a queue in memory of at most 10,000 events. Past that it drops the oldest, so an outage cannot fill your server's memory.

- It sends batches of up to 50: as soon as 50 are waiting, and every 5 seconds otherwise.

- Each request gets 10 seconds. No answer, a `5xx`, a `429` or a `409 site_changed` is retried up to five times, waiting about 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 seconds or the `Retry-After` the answer gives, then the batch waits in the queue for the next flush. Any other `4xx` is logged and never retried.

- Every retry sends the same body, so the same `eventId` values.

- When collection pauses, it keeps the refused events and sends them on a later flush. It logs any conflicts.

- It reads the key from the `RAVENSIGHT_SERVER_KEY` environment variable, and checks names, actor ids and properties against the rules above when you call `track`, so a mistake throws in your code instead of failing a whole batch later.

- `close()` sends what is left when your process shuts down.



server-events.mjs



```
// server-events.mjs: send Ravensight server events from Node 18 or newer.
// No dependencies. The server key stays on your server, in an environment variable.
import https from 'node:https';
import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';

const NAME = /^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_.-]{0,79}$/;
const ACTOR = /^[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{8,128}$/;
const KEY = /^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$/;
// Property keys the server keeps for itself, in any letter case.
const RESERVED_PREFIX = /^(?:rs_|history_|ravensight_)/i;
const RESERVED_KEYS = ['context', 'identity_kind', 'session_provenance', 'profile_provenance'];
const reserved = key => RESERVED_PREFIX.test(key) || RESERVED_KEYS.includes(key.toLowerCase());
// String values the server refuses because they look like a person or a credential.
const CREDENTIAL = /@|%40|(?:bearer|password|secret|token|authorization)[=:]|(?:^|\s)bearer\s/i;
const TOKEN = /^eyJ[\w-]+\.|\bgt_[a-z]+_[\w-]{8,}|[\w-]{80,}/;
const sleep = ms => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

export class ServerEvents {
  constructor({
    key = process.env.RAVENSIGHT_SERVER_KEY,
    url = 'https://api.ravensight.io/api/v1/site/server-events',
    maxQueue = 10000,  // past this, the oldest events are dropped
    batchSize = 50,    // the most one request may carry
    flushMs = 5000,    // send what is queued this often
    timeoutMs = 10000, // give up on one request after this long
    retries = 5,       // tries after the first, then the batch waits for the next flush
    backoffMs = 1000,  // the first wait between tries; it doubles, up to 30 seconds
    log = console.warn,
  } = {}) {
    if (!key?.startsWith('gt_srv_')) {
      throw new Error('Set RAVENSIGHT_SERVER_KEY to the website server key');
    }
    Object.assign(this, { key, maxQueue, timeoutMs, retries, backoffMs, log });
    this.url = new URL(url);
    this.batchSize = Math.min(batchSize, 50);
    this.queue = [];
    this.flushing = null;
    this.timer = setInterval(() => this.flush(), flushMs);
    this.timer.unref(); // the timer alone never keeps the process running
  }

  // Queue one event and return its eventId. actorId is your own opaque id, never an email.
  track(name, { actorId, properties, timestamp = Date.now() } = {}) {
    if (!NAME.test(name)) throw new Error(`Invalid event name: ${name}`);
    if (actorId !== undefined && !ACTOR.test(actorId)) {
      throw new Error('actorId must be 8 to 128 of A-Z a-z 0-9 _ . : -');
    }
    if (properties !== undefined) checkProperties(properties);
    const event = { eventId: randomUUID(), name, timestamp };
    if (actorId !== undefined) event.actorId = actorId;
    if (properties !== undefined) event.properties = properties;
    this.queue.push(event);
    this.trim();
    if (this.queue.length >= this.batchSize) this.flush();
    return event.eventId;
  }

  // Send everything queued. Only one flush runs at a time.
  flush() {
    this.flushing ??= this.drain()
      .catch(error => this.log(`Ravensight flush failed: ${error.message}`))
      .finally(() => { this.flushing = null; });
    return this.flushing;
  }

  // Stop the timer and send what is left. Call it before the process exits.
  async close(timeoutMs = 8000) {
    clearInterval(this.timer);
    this.retries = Math.min(this.retries, 1); // shutting down: two tries at most
    const deadline = new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, timeoutMs).unref());
    await Promise.race([this.flush(), deadline]);
    if (this.queue.length) this.log(`${this.queue.length} Ravensight events were not sent`);
  }

  trim() {
    const over = this.queue.length - this.maxQueue;
    if (over > 0) {
      this.queue.splice(0, over);
      this.log(`Ravensight queue full: dropped the ${over} oldest events`);
    }
  }

  async drain() {
    while (this.queue.length) {
      const batch = this.queue.splice(0, this.batchSize);
      const later = await this.send(batch);
      if (later.length) { // keep them, oldest first, for the next flush
        this.queue.unshift(...later);
        this.trim();
        return;
      }
    }
  }

  // Send one batch. Returns the events to keep for a later flush.
  async send(batch) {
    const body = JSON.stringify({ events: batch }); // the same eventIds on every try
    for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
      const res = await this.post(body).catch(error => ({ status: 0, error }));
      const answer = res.json || {};
      if (answer.conflicts) {
        // An eventId reused with other content: only those events were refused.
        this.log('Ravensight refused reused eventIds:', answer.conflictIds);
      }
      if (res.status === 200 && !answer.paused) return [];
      if (answer.paused) {
        // The balance cannot cover more events: keep the refused ones for a later flush.
        this.log(`Ravensight collection paused (${answer.reason || 'needs_funds'})`);
        const refused = new Set(answer.refusedIds);
        return batch.filter(event => refused.has(event.eventId));
      }
      const retry = res.status === 0 || res.status === 429 || res.status >= 500
        || (res.status === 409 && answer.error === 'site_changed');
      if (!retry) {
        // Any other 4xx would fail the same way again: log it and drop the batch.
        this.log(`Ravensight refused ${batch.length} events:`, res.status, answer.error ?? '');
        return [];
      }
      // No answer, a 5xx, a 429 or site_changed: wait, then send the same body again.
      if (attempt >= this.retries) return batch;
      const backoff = Math.min(this.backoffMs * 2 ** attempt, 30000);
      const jittered = backoff * (0.5 + Math.random() / 2);
      await sleep(res.retryAfter ? Math.min(res.retryAfter, 60) * 1000 : jittered);
    }
  }

  post(body) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const req = https.request(this.url, {
        method: 'POST',
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(this.timeoutMs),
        headers: {
          authorization: `Bearer ${this.key}`,
          'content-type': 'application/json',
          'content-length': Buffer.byteLength(body),
        },
      }, res => {
        let text = '';
        res.setEncoding('utf8');
        res.on('data', chunk => { text += chunk; });
        res.on('error', reject);
        res.on('close', () => {
          if (!res.complete) reject(new Error('response cut off'));
        });
        res.on('end', () => {
          let json = null;
          try { json = JSON.parse(text); } catch {}
          const retryAfter = Number(res.headers['retry-after']) || 0;
          resolve({ status: res.statusCode, json, retryAfter });
        });
      });
      req.on('error', reject);
      req.end(body);
    });
  }
}

function checkProperties(properties) {
  const entries = Object.entries(properties);
  const safe = text => text.length <= 200 && !CREDENTIAL.test(text) && !TOKEN.test(text);
  const valid = value => value === null || typeof value === 'boolean'
    || Number.isFinite(value) || (typeof value === 'string' && safe(value));
  const bad = entries.find(([key, value]) => !KEY.test(key) || reserved(key) || !valid(value));
  if (bad) throw new Error(`Invalid property: ${bad[0]}`);
  if (entries.length > 24 || Buffer.byteLength(JSON.stringify(properties)) > 4096) {
    throw new Error('At most 24 properties and 4 KB');
  }
}
```



In your server



```
import { ServerEvents } from './server-events.mjs';

const events = new ServerEvents(); // reads RAVENSIGHT_SERVER_KEY

// After your own code has created the account:
events.track('account_created', {
  actorId: account.id, // your own opaque id, never an email
  properties: { plan: 'free', method: 'password' },
});

// Send what is left before the process exits.
for (const signal of ['SIGTERM', 'SIGINT']) {
  process.once(signal, async () => {
    await events.close();
    process.exit(0);
  });
}
```



The queue lives in memory, so events still waiting when the process is killed without warning are lost. For an event that must never be lost, keep it in your own database and send it from there with a stable id, as above.

### JavaScript game canvas



```
// After the player chooses to attach an image, preview it in your UI.
const screenshot = await rs.captureScreenshot(gameCanvas);
const report = await rs.createReport({
  message: 'The cave door stays locked after collecting three keys.',
  category: 'bug', level: 'cave', screenshot,
  context: { keys: 3 }
});
const receipt = await rs.submitReport(report);
// If processing, check again later from your UI, not in a tight loop.
const status = await rs.getReportStatus(report);
// status.status === 'complete': status.reportId is in Player reports.
```



Capture is opt-in and limited to the supplied game canvas. It does not capture the desktop. You may instead pass your own PNG, JPEG or WebP bytes. Keep the prepared report object for retries on the same SDK instance; do not create a new ID after a timeout. The SDK keeps the original session, never silently submits a second report under a new session, and never silently replaces a failed screenshot with text. A text-only report is an explicit player choice.



Images must be at most 1,000,000 bytes and 4,000,000 pixels. Capture helpers scale within 1600×1600. Screenshot hashing in the browser requires HTTPS or localhost. Reports count as one event plus 20,000 events for the image. Unfinished uploads expire after 15 minutes; failed or canceled images release reserved usage. Images are retained for 90 days, subject to delivery limits, and are not automatically sent to AI providers.

### Worked examples



Each example is one session, read under the default detection profile (a death is a failure, rage quit on, no terminal events), with every event inside the queried window. The outcome is what the code computes, word for word where it writes an indicator.



#### Level start



```
1  game_started    {}
2  level_started   {"level": "cavern-1"}
3  boss_entered    {"level": "cavern-1"}
4  shop_entered    {"level": "cavern-1", "rs_kind": "none"}
5  level_started   {}
```



The level funnel shows `cavern-1` with 2 starts: line 2, and line 3, because `boss_entered` contains `enter`. Line 4 would match too, but `rs_kind: "none"` opts it out. Lines 1 and 5 are level starts by name (`game_started` contains `start`) but carry no place, so the funnel never sees them. With no completion, `cavern-1` reads 0 percent complete.



#### Level complete



```
1  level_started   {"level": "1-1"}
2  level_complete  {"level": "1-1"}
3  level_started   {"track": "1-2"}
4  stage_cleared   {"location": "1-2"}
5  window_opened   {"level": "1-2"}
```



`1-1`: 1 start, 1 completion, 100 percent. `1-2`: 1 start (line 3, where `track` is the place) and 2 completions, line 4 (`cleared` contains `clear`, and `location` is the place) and line 5, because `window_opened` contains `win`. Two completions against one start is a completion rate of 2, so send line 5 with `rs_kind: "none"`.



#### Death



```
1  level_started   {"level": "boss"}
2  player_died     {"level": "boss"}
3  ship_destroyed  {"level": "boss"}
4  enemy_killed    {"level": "boss"}
5  run_ended       {"level": "boss", "rs_kind": "death"}
```



The session's death count is 4, and the funnel shows `boss` with 1 start, 0 completions and 4 deaths: lines 2, 3 and 4 by name, line 5 by its `rs_kind`. Line 4 is the trap: `enemy_killed` contains `killed`, so an enemy's death counts as the player's unless it is sent with `rs_kind: "none"`. The rage quit rules read only `died` and `destroyed` names (plus `rs_kind: "death"`), so for them lines 2, 3 and 5 are deaths and line 4 is not; that is 3 deaths in the last 5 events and 3 at `boss`, so this session is also flagged rage quit likely.



#### Rage quit



```
1  level_started   {"level": "3"}
2  player_died     {}
3  player_died     {}
4  hint_shown      {}
5  player_died     {}
```



Flagged rage quit likely with the indicator `3 deaths in last 5 events`. The same place rule does not fire: deaths with no place are left out of it, so a placeless death is never counted as being at any place. Sent with `{"level": "3"}`, the same three deaths would add `3 deaths at same location (3)`. The last event is a death whose name contains `died`, so a query whose window ends more than 10 minutes after line 5 also adds `Session ended immediately after death (Nmin ago)`, with N the whole minutes since that event; a last event named `ship_destroyed` would not, because that rule reads only `died` names and `rs_kind: "death"`.



#### Stuck



```
1  level_started   {"level": "ledge"}
2  jump_failed     {"level": "ledge"}   sent 10 times
3  hint_shown      {}
4  jump_failed     {}                   sent 4 times
5  game_exited     {}
```



17 events in all. Flagged potentially stuck with the indicator `Repeating same action: jump_failed (10x)`. The rule is one event name repeated 10 or more times at one place, in a session of fewer than 20 events (19 at most). Repeats are counted per event name and place, and events with no place share one group of their own: the 4 placeless `jump_failed` on line 4 are counted apart from the 10 at `ledge`, and had all 14 been sent without a place they would have counted as 14 at that one placeless group. Three more events of any kind would make 20, and the session would not be flagged.

### What is kept



For each click Ravensight keeps:



- the time, the game, campaign and link, and the source, medium and destination type you chose;

- the referring site's domain only, never the full referring address. The Clicks tab lists the top ten, then the clicks with no referring site, then the rest as one row;

- a device class: phone, desktop, tablet or other;

- whether it was filtered, and whether it was billed.



No IP address is stored. No fingerprint is built. The only thing that touches the connecting address is a short-lived keyed hash used to spot a burst from one visitor, which is rotated daily, cannot be queried and is not written to the click record. Click records are kept for 90 days, like events, and deleting a game deletes its campaigns, links and clicks.

### From the REST data API, through a Google Sheet



Looker Studio has no built-in connector that sends an `Authorization` header to an arbitrary API, so this route writes the answer into a Google Sheet that Looker Studio then reads.



1. Create a Google Sheet and open **Extensions**, **Apps Script**.

1. In the script's **Project settings**, add a script property named `RAVENSIGHT_TOKEN` holding your token, so the token is not in the code.

1. Paste the script below, replace `GAME_ID`, and run `loadRevenue` once to grant it access.

1. Add a time-driven trigger for `loadRevenue` (once a day is one request a day).

1. In Looker Studio, add the Google Sheets connector and choose this sheet.



```
function loadRevenue() {
  const token = PropertiesService.getScriptProperties().getProperty('RAVENSIGHT_TOKEN');
  const response = UrlFetchApp.fetch('https://api.ravensight.io/api/v1/data/games/GAME_ID/monetization', {
    headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer ' + token }
  });
  const body = JSON.parse(response.getContentText());
  const rows = [['date', 'currency', 'revenue_minor_units', 'minor_unit_digits']];
  body.byCurrency.forEach(function (currency) {
    currency.series.forEach(function (day) {
      rows.push([day.date, currency.currency, day.revenue, currency.minorUnitDigits]);
    });
  });
  const sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActive().getSheets()[0];
  sheet.clearContents();
  sheet.getRange(1, 1, rows.length, rows[0].length).setValues(rows);
}
```

### Granting S3 access



Ravensight writes as one role and never holds a key of yours. Add this statement to your bucket policy, with the role ARN and studio id the export dialog shows you. The condition pins the role's session name, which ends in your studio's id, so only Ravensight writing on behalf of your studio can use it. It allows writing new objects under your prefix and nothing else: Ravensight never lists, reads or deletes anything in your bucket. Creating the export makes one test write, `<prefix>/_ravensight_test/<time>.json`, and reports access denied or a missing bucket by name. Connection tests are limited to once per minute for each destination.



```
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Sid": "RavensightExport",
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": { "AWS": "<role ARN from the dialog>" },
    "Action": "s3:PutObject",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::<your-bucket>/<prefix>/*",
    "Condition": { "StringLike": { "aws:userid": "*:ravensight-export-<studio id>" } }
  }]
}
```



If the bucket encrypts with your own KMS key, name the key in the dialog and allow the same role `kms:GenerateDataKey` on it; otherwise objects are written with S3-managed encryption. We recommend the bucket's Object Ownership setting "Bucket owner enforced" (the default for new buckets), so every exported file is owned by you with no access control lists to manage.
